TASK 1 - DBMS & SQL BASICS
==========================

Q1. Define Data.
Ans: Data refers to unprocessed facts, figures, symbols or values gathered about a
     person, object or event. On its own it carries no meaning; once it is stored and
     processed it turns into useful information.

Q2. Define a Database.
Ans: A database is a systematically arranged collection of related data kept together
     in one place so that it can be searched, updated and retrieved without difficulty.
     In simple words - it is a storehouse where data is kept in an organised form.

Q3. What do you mean by DBMS?
Ans: DBMS stands for Database Management System. It is the software layer that sits
     between the user and the database and allows us to build, store, arrange, modify
     and fetch data. Examples: MySQL, Oracle, MS Access, MongoDB.

Q4. What do you mean by RDBMS?
Ans: RDBMS stands for Relational Database Management System. It is a category of DBMS
     in which data is kept in the form of tables (rows and columns) and one table can
     be linked with another through relationships.

Q5. Distinguish between DBMS and RDBMS.
Ans:
     Basis            | DBMS                          | RDBMS
     -----------------|-------------------------------|--------------------------------
     Full form        | Database Management System    | Relational DBMS
     Data storage     | File, hierarchical or other   | Strictly in table format
                      | formats                       |
     Relationship     | Not compulsory                | Tables are linked using keys
     Normalization    | Generally not applied         | Applied to reduce redundancy
     Data volume      | Fits small / simple systems   | Handles large, structured data
     Keys             | Rarely used                   | Primary & Foreign keys used
     Examples         | MS Access, file systems       | MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL

     Short point: DBMS only manages data, whereas RDBMS manages data along with the
     relationships between tables.

Q6. What is SQL?
Ans: SQL is short for Structured Query Language. It is the standard language through
     which we talk to a relational database - creating tables, inserting values,
     fetching records, updating them and removing them.

Q7. State the benefits of maintaining a Database.
Ans: 1) Data stays in a neat and structured form.
     2) Required information can be located quickly.
     3) A very large volume of data can be kept in one place.
     4) Accuracy and consistency of data is maintained.
     5) Unauthorised users can be kept away from the data.
     6) Adding, changing or removing data becomes simple.

Q8. State the benefits of a DBMS.
Ans: 1) Security - decides which user is allowed to see or change what.
     2) Integrity - keeps the stored values valid and correct.
     3) Less redundancy - avoids storing the same data again and again.
     4) Backup & recovery - data can be restored if it gets damaged or lost.
     5) Concurrent access - many users can work on the data at the same time.
     6) Simplified handling - insert, update, delete and search operations become easy.

Q9. State the benefits of an RDBMS.
Ans: 1) Data is arranged neatly in rows and columns.
     2) Related tables can be joined using Primary Key and Foreign Key.
     3) Normalization cuts down repeated data.
     4) Constraints such as PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY and NOT NULL protect data validity.
     5) SQL queries make searching and analysis easy.
     6) A change made in one table can be reflected properly across related tables.

Q10. What is a Table?
Ans: A table is the basic storage unit of a relational database in which related data
     is arranged horizontally in rows and vertically in columns.

Q11. What is a Record?
Ans: A record holds the complete details of a single entity - one student, one employee,
     one product. In a table, one record = one row (also called a tuple).

Q12. What is a Field?
Ans: A field represents one particular attribute or property of the data, for example
     Name, Age or City. In a table, one field = one column.

Q13. Explain Primary Key.
Ans: A Primary Key is that column, or set of columns, whose value is different for every
     row and is never left blank. It is used to identify each record uniquely. Only one
     Primary Key is permitted per table.

Q14. Explain Foreign Key.
Ans: A Foreign Key is a column of one table which points to the Primary Key of some other
     table. It is the element that actually builds the relationship between two tables.

Q15. What is meant by a Constraint?
Ans: Constraints are the conditions or rules applied on a column (or on the whole table)
     that restrict the kind of values which may be entered, so that wrong data does not
     get stored.

Q16. List the constraints available in SQL.
Ans: NOT NULL, UNIQUE, PRIMARY KEY, FOREIGN KEY, CHECK, DEFAULT and AUTO_INCREMENT.

Q17. Why is the PRIMARY KEY constraint used?
Ans: To recognise every row of a table uniquely; it does not allow duplicate or NULL
     values.

Q18. Why is the FOREIGN KEY constraint used?
Ans: To join one table with another and to make sure the referenced value really exists
     in the parent table.

Q19. Why is the NOT NULL constraint used?
Ans: To make a column compulsory, i.e. the field cannot be left empty.

Q20. Why is the UNIQUE constraint used?
Ans: To stop the same value from being entered twice in a column.

Q21. Why is the CHECK constraint used?
Ans: To allow only those values which satisfy a given condition, e.g. CHECK (Age >= 18).

Q22. Why is the DEFAULT constraint used?
Ans: To fill a pre-decided value automatically whenever the user does not supply one.

Q23. What are SQL Commands?
Ans: SQL commands are the instructions given to the database engine to build its
     structure and to work with the data kept inside it.

Q24. Explain DDL with its commands.
Ans: DDL means Data Definition Language. It deals with the structure or design of
     database objects such as databases, tables and views - not with the values inside.
     Commands: CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE, RENAME.

Q25. Explain DML with its commands.
Ans: DML means Data Manipulation Language. It works on the actual data present inside
     the table.
     Commands: INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE.

Q26. Explain DQL with its command.
Ans: DQL means Data Query Language. It is used only to fetch or display the stored data.
     Command: SELECT.

Q27. Differentiate between DELETE, TRUNCATE and DROP.
Ans:
     DELETE   - Removes selected rows; a WHERE clause can be used; table remains.
     TRUNCATE - Wipes out every row at once, but the empty table structure stays.
     DROP     - Destroys the entire object (table/database) along with its data.

Q28. Differentiate between PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE KEY.
Ans:
     PRIMARY KEY - Identifies a row uniquely, NULL is not allowed, only one per table.
     UNIQUE KEY  - Only prevents duplicate entries, NULL is allowed, a table may have
                   many unique keys.
